s on a draft of just such a  blueprint with military concision he  methodically assessed the international  situation laid out several scenarios  based on different objectives and  articulated what he and the other  members of the quartet believed was  America's prime objective finally he  weighed the country's capabilities in  light of that objective taking into  account not only the level of the  military's readiness but also what he  called the political realities in our  own country his aim he unpretentiously  told Frank Knox was merely to shed some  light upon the major decisions that the  President may make for guiding our  future military and naval efforts in the  event of a war and in further immediate  preparations for war it was a very  humble goal for a document that outlined  the course that the United States would  follow for the next five years through  the utterly unforeseen events of the  most destructive war in human history  stark wrote his report himself on an  ancient device technically technically  known as a manual typewriter it was  double spaced and 26 pages long  written and non-aggressive everyday  language on the first page he explained  that his intention was to present his  views concerning steps we might take to  meet the situation that will exist if  the United States enters the war either  alone or with allies and then he posed  the crucial question where should we  fight the war and for what objective and  he offered four different scenarios plan  a proposed that the United States  maintain a purely defensive posture in  the Western Hemisphere and avoid any  involvement in the war for as long as  possible neither in the East nor the  West would we do anything more than  provide material help to America's  allies it was a plan that would do  nothing extraordinary to head to a  Briton even though the quartet believed  that the survival of Britain was  essential to European security Stark's  plan be laid out a strictly defensive  posture in the Atlantic but a full  offensive against Japan in the Pacific  he explained that this option placed  full confidence in the British to hold  their own indefinitely in the Atlantic  while the US would focus on defeating  Japan but he also proposed that if Great  Britain was losing in its struggle  against Nazi Germany the US would have  to immediately reorient toward the  Atlantic here comes Plan C this was the  nightmare scenario it envisaged a  two-front war with the u.s. sending the  strongest possible military assistance  to Britain as well as to the Far East  the handicap of fighting two major wars  in two theatres at the same time was of  course mind boggling stark acknowledged  that such a two-front war would disperse  US forces and virtually preclude  successful operations in either theater  the last scenario was planned d  it had the United States directing its  efforts toward a strong offensive in the  Atlantic as an ally of Britain while  maintaining only a defensive position  against Japan in the Pacific plan D  stipulated that if the United States was  forced into a war with Japan it would  still avoid major operations in the  Pacific until the Atlantic front and  Britain were entirely secure of course  circumstances would ultimately determine  American actions but taking all probable  factors into account stark firmly  recommended that the Roosevelt  administration adopt plan D he rejected  the other options as counterproductive  and he gave four reasons why first the  secure existence of Britain and its  Empire would do the most to ensure the  security of the United States and the  Western Hemisphere second it was Germany  and not Japan that most seriously  threatened Britain security and even its  survival third therefore it was Germany  that also posed the greatest danger to  the security of the United States and  finally for stark wrote that in the  interest of American and British  security America's full offensive  strength must be exerted in one single  direction and Atlantic first and a  defeat Germany strategy the adoption of  planned dog or plan sorry a plan D or  planned dog as it was called a naval  parlance would mean that the fate of the  United States would be inextricably  bound to that of Britain that we would  do everything we could to meet Britain's  vital needs and that American soldiers  might have to fight in a land war in  Europe while Roosevelt was promising  that your boys are not going to be sent  into any foreign wars Admiral Stark was  already envisioning American  participation in a full-scale land  offensive in Europe of course stark  knew that the United States was still  building up its own military strength  and wasn't yet ready to engage in  offensive actions and so he advised that  for the very near future Roosevelt adopt  plan a hemispheric defense with only  material aid to the Allies Roosevelt's  response to the planned dog memorandum  with something of a mystery he never  signaled outright approval but he also  gave no sign of disapproval and so  Marshall Stimson Knox and stark adopted  the Atlantic first strategy because the  president didn't say they couldn't FDR's  tacit acquiescence left the quartet free  to conclude that military strategy would  be left to the leaders of the military  the credit goes to Admiral Stark it was  he who seized the initiative and fought  to bring order and coherence to what had  been a muddle and above all it was he  who drafted a plan that was true to both  the shape of the world crisis and to the  potential leadership role of the United  States even though in 1942 and most of  43 more American forces were in fact  deployed against Japan than against  Germany plan dog nevertheless defined  America's paramount wartime strategy and  goals in fact it was the fundamental US  strategic decision of the war now with  the goal of defending Britain and  defeating germany how would the  Americans and the British coordinate  their planning and their actions the  last crucial recommendation in Starks  memo was that members of the American  military senior staff hold secret talks  that winter in Washington with their  British counterparts as a preliminary  two possible American entry into the war  general Marshall completely agreed with  the necessity of such meetings  after the war he wrote if we hadn't had  some talks with the British we would  have started with no basis at all if we  entered the war we needed to have at  least a vague idea of what we could  expect from the British and what sort of  help they would want from us another  reason for proposing join talks was that  both Marshall and Stark had serious  misgivings about the competence of  British military leaders in fact they  feared that the American military effort  would be sabotaged by British ineptitude  and miscalculation ironically Prime  Minister Churchill had no objections to  the low opinion that American America's  top military of officers held of the  fitness of the British for battle the  Prime Minister reasoned that the more  floundering the Britain appeared the  more likely it was that the United  States would rush to her aid Churchill  wrote that it was particularly helpful  that American military leaders  considered Britain incapable of winning  alone Roosevelt approved the talks but  demanded that they be kept absolutely  secret one of his advisers commented  that if the Germans and Japanese had  learned of the talks no great damage  would have been done because they would  have expected as much but had the  isolationists in Congress found out then  American preparations for war might have  been wrecked Roosevelt began a mid  January meeting at the White House with  marshal Stimson knox and stark by  soberly laying out the very grave  situation he said that there was one  chance in five the Germany would invade  Britain immediately in which case  Britain might survive for six months at  best he emphasized the importance of  keeping the British supplied with all  they needed even in the event of an axis  attack on the unite  States the US he said would simply have  to be ready to act with what we have  available then he outlined the position  he wanted the Americans to take at the  joint talks in other words these were  their marching orders one we would stand  on the defensive in the Pacific with the  fleet based on Hawaii to the Navy should  be prepared to convoy shipping in the  Atlantic to England three the army  should not engage in offensive action  until it was fully prepared for our  military course must be conservative  until our strength is fully developed  five we will make every effort to supply  war materiel to Great Britain this was  Admiral Starks memorandum in a nutshell  Roosevelt didn't mention plan dog by  name but his directions could be  interpreted as a comprehensive  endorsement on January 24th 1941 the  Royal Navy ship King George the fifth  arrived in Chesapeake Bay newspapers  reported that on board was the new  British ambassador to the US Lord  Halifax but they didn't report that also  arriving were representatives of the  British Chiefs of Staff they were  camouflaged in civilian clothes and  their assignment was to participate in  high-level confidential discussions with  their American counterparts to create a  joint strategy for war five days later  the American British conversations or  ABC got underway and would continue for  two months until the end of March the  Americans opened the conversations with  a statement of the u.s. position at the  present time the United States would  remain out of the war but it would  provide all material and economic aid to  Great Britain but if the United States  was compelled to resort to war it was  committed to the defeat of Germany and  Atlantic first strategy the US was also  committed to the containment of Japan  but even if it had to conduct military  operations against Japan it would limit  those operations so as not to diminish  America's main effort in the Atlantic it  was once again plan dog in a nutshell  the British accepted nearly all of the  American state of the American statement  and goals especially the number one  priority of defeating germany but they  clashed over Britain's determination to  maintain control over its global empire  and protect its interests in the Far  East from India and Burma to Australia  and New Zealand the British  representatives at the abc talks  believed that the defense of those  interests hinged entirely on their naval  base in Singapore and that the loss of  Singapore would be a disaster the bottom  line was that the British wanted the  Americans to send a fleet to Singapore  but the Americans didn't want to do the  dirty work of salvaging the British  Empire and so the subject of Singapore  provoked a bitter stalemate in the talks  when Prime Minister Churchill heard  about his staff's intransigent position  on Singapore he blew his stack what has  been the use I wish I could say this  with his accident I can't what has been  the use of all this battling he said our  sole object is to get the Americans into  the war if they prefer Hawaii to  Singapore there is no more to be said  despite this conflict in other clashes  the talks brought substantial agreements  Britain was assured of American help in  the Atlantic and there was no argument  about the importance of defeating Nazi  Germany first the Brits accepted that  the American presence in the Pacific  would remain a defensive one with its  main fleet stationed in Hawaii as a  deterrent to Japanese aggression the ABC  talks were  clear win for the United States but  above all planned dog had become and  would remain the operative strategy for  the United States and for Britain one  senior British staff member called it  the Bible of our joint collaboration and  not even Pearl Harbor would change that  we know the disputes between Britain and  the United States over strategic policy  would continue the two countries would  calculate and plot their decisions  according to their own national and  imperial interests and once Americans  were in the war Churchill would not  always be as yielding as he was during  the ABC talks but the talks had created  a partnership that could withstand the  bitterest of disagreements to come and  that would soon evolve into permanent  joint military and naval missions in  London and Washington with staffs  numbering in the hundreds for his part  Winston Churchill was elated about the  new era that was dawning after the war  he wrote we were conscious of the  tremendous changes taking place in  American opinion and of the growing  belief not only in Washington but  throughout the country that their fate  was bound up with ours and in March 1941  nine months before Pearl Harbor the new  Anglo American alliance was  indestructible anchored on both sides of  the Atlantic I'll conclude by saying  that although the United States remained  at the moment in a gray area neither a  pea snort war plan dog and the ABC talks  demonstrate that in late nineteen forty  in early 41 despite all his caution  Roosevelt grasp the likely course of  events and thanks to prodding by  Marshall Stimson knox and stark he  approved crucial preparations for war  during his third hundred days he made no  public declaration of his intentions  his thoughts about the inevitability of  American involvement in the global  conflict but plans for war we're now  unmistakeably and irrevocably underway  thank you
 

